// 24_multi_row_full.mty — full multi-row-var typecheck (v0.19).
//
// HIR + typeck now propagate every row var; caller's effect set
// is the union of closure effects. Where examples/23_multi_row.mty
// (v0.18) pinned the multi-row-var PARSER surface, this example
// proves the END-TO-END pipeline:
//
//   1. Parser (v0.18): `!{| E, F}` produces one EFFECT_ROW_TAIL
//      with two EFFECT_ROW_VAR children.
//   2. HIR lowering (v0.19, this slice): every EFFECT_ROW_VAR
//      lands in `Vec<HirRowVar>` (idx 0 for E, idx 1 for F) —
//      v0.18 dropped F at this step.
//   3. Typeck (v0.17): `UserRowPolyMeta::row_vars` reports both
//      names; the per-call-site walker unions every closure-arg's
//      effects into the caller's inferred set.
//
// Build:
//   mty fmt examples/24_multi_row_full.mty
//   mty check examples/24_multi_row_full.mty
//
// (`_` prefix on `_cross` keeps the helper out of the WIT export
// world — the example focus is the SIGNATURE shape, not
// exportability; mirrors the convention used by 22_effect_row /
// 23_multi_row.)
// A row-poly fn with TWO row variables, each bound by an
// independent fn-typed parameter. The return-row clause `!{| E, F}`
// declares the union — at each call site, `E` resolves to closure
// `a`'s effects and `F` to closure `b`'s, and the caller inherits
// both via RFC-008 §inference.
fn _cross[E, F](
  a: fn() -> Unit,
  b: fn() -> Unit,
) -> Unit !{| E, F} {
}

fn main() {
  // The signature `!{| E, F}` is the focus of this example —
  // typecheck happily proves a hypothetical caller's effect set
  // would be the union of both closure effects. Codegen for
  // higher-order calls with two closure args is exercised by
  // unit tests rather than the example suite (it would otherwise
  // duplicate the v0.15 stdlib HOF coverage). Mirror the v0.18
  // example 23 pattern: declare the row-poly fn, leave the
  // multi-arg HOF call to the test suite.
  log("24_multi_row_full: RFC-008 multi-row-var lowering completeness (see _cross)")
}
